    "value": "*Desktop/Mobile:*\n\n# Given a shopper visits any page on the storefront, when the page loads, then the global header is displayed at the top of the page on both desktop and mobile.\n# Given a shopper scrolls down the page, when the page is scrolled, then the top navigation remains sticky and visible at the top of the screen.\n# Given a shopper clicks the Zimmermann logo, when the click is registered, then the shopper is navigated to the homepage.\n# Given a shopper has items in their bag, when they view the header, then the Bag icon displays the correct running count of items.\n# Given a bag item is added or removed, when the bag is updated, then the Bag icon count updates without requiring a full page reload.\n\n*Desktop:*\n\n# Given a shopper views the header, when the page loads, then all header icons (Search, Account, Saved, Bag) are visible per the linked Figma Design.\n\n*Mobile:*\n\n# Given a shopper views the site on mobile, when any page loads, then the hamburger icon is displayed top-left in the header alongside Search and Bag icons top-right.\n# Given a shopper taps the hamburger icon, when the tap is registered, then the full-screen navigation drawer opens and the hamburger icon is replaced by a close (×) icon.\n# Given the navigation drawer is open, when a shopper taps the close (×) icon, then the drawer closes and the hamburger icon is restored.\n# Given the navigation drawer is open, when a shopper views it, then all top-level navigation categories are displayed vertically in the correct order per the linked Figma design.\n# Given the navigation drawer is open, when a shopper views it, then Saved and Account links are displayed below the main navigation categories."
  }
}"}

content.content:
{"result":"{
  "id": "19197",
  "key": "SFCC-746",
  "summary": "Navigation – PWA Kit & Contentful",
  "customfield_10231": {
    "value": "*Content Type*\n\n# Given the Contentful content type is created, when a Site Merchandiser opens the content model, then it includes nav item label, category ID, display order, and storefront scoping.\n# Given a Site Merchandiser updates and publishes the nav entries in Contentful, when the page loads after publishing, then the updated nav items and ordering are reflected across all storefronts without a code deployment.\n\n*Structure — Desktop*\n\n# Given a shopper visits any page on desktop, when the page loads, then the Navigation component renders with top-level nav items displayed left-aligned in the configured order.\n# Given a shopper visits any page on desktop, when the page loads, then Search Bar, Account, Wishlist and Bag icons are displayed right-aligned in the nav bar.\n# Given a shopper opens a mega menu on desktop, when the mega menu is active, then the four icons are replaced by a CLOSE X control on the right side of the nav\n# Given a shopper clicks CLOSE X on desktop, when the control is activated, then the mega menu is dismissed and the four icons are restored.\n\n*Structure — Tablet*\n\n# Given a shopper visits any page on tablet, when the page loads, then the Navigation component renders with all top-level nav items and all four icons visible (Search, Account, Wishlist, Bag)\n# Given a shopper opens a mega menu on tablet, when the mega menu is active, then the four icons are replaced by CLOSE X on the right side of the nav.\n# Given a shopper clicks CLOSE X on tablet, when the control is activated, then the mega menu is dismissed and the four icons are restored.\n\n*Structure — Mobile*\n\n# Given a shopper visits any page on mobile, when the page loads, then the Navigation component renders with a hamburger icon on the left and Search and Bag icons on the right. Top-level nav items are not visible.\n# Given a shopper opens the mobile drawer, when the drawer is active, then the hamburger icon is replaced by a close icon on the left. Search and Bag icons remain visible on the right.\n# Given a shopper taps the close icon on mobile, when activated, then the mobile drawer is dismissed and the hamburger icon is restored.\n\n*Category Taxonomy*\n\n# Given the Navigation component renders, when it reads top-level nav items, then items, their display order, and storefront visibility are managed in Contentful using a category ID to reference the SFCC category taxonomy in BM (SFCC-28) and are not hardcoded in the component.\n# Given a nav item corresponds to an SFCC category, when a shopper clicks it, then they are navigated to the corresponding category PLP.\n# Given the Navigation component fetches top-level nav items from Contentful, when it renders, then it uses the storefront-specific configuration ensuring nav items and their ordering reflect the correct regional setup.\n# Given a nav item is unpublished or not configured in Contentful for a specific storefront, when the Navigation component renders on that storefront, then the corresponding nav item does not appear in the headline nav.\n\n*Search*\n\n# Given a shopper clicks the search icon in the header, when the interaction occurs, then they are navigated to the search experience - [https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/browse/SFCC-113|https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/browse/SFCC-113|smart-link] \n\n*Wishlist*\n\n# Given a shopper views the header and their wishlist is empty, when the component renders, then the wishlist icon displays in its outlined state.\n# Given a shopper views the header and their wishlist contains one or more items, when the component renders, then the wishlist icon displays in its filled state.\n\n*Cart*\n\n# Given a shopper views the header, when the cart contains items, then the cart icon displays a dynamic item count — full cart behaviour is covered in the dynamic cart header story - [https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/browse/SFCC-827|https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/browse/SFCC-827|smart-link] \n\n*Accessibility*\n\n# Given a screen reader user is navigating by landmarks, when they reach the main navigation, then it is wrapped in a nav element with aria-label equal to Main navigation, distinguishing it from any other nav regions on the page.\n# Given a screen reader user navigates the headline nav, when they move through the top-level items, then each item is announced correctly and the number of items is communicated via a list structure.\n# Given a keyboard-only user is navigating the header on desktop or tablet, when they Tab through the headline nav, then each top-level item and icon receives focus in left-to-right order.\n# Given a screen reader user navigates to a top-level item that triggers a mega menu, when the item receives focus and the menu is closed, then the item is announced with aria-haspopup true and aria-expanded false.\n# Given a screen reader user activates a mega menu trigger, when the menu opens, then aria-expanded updates to true and the CLOSE X control is focusable and announced as Close menu.\n# Given a screen reader user navigates to Z.IMMERSE, when the item receives focus, then it is announced as a standard link with no aria-haspopup or aria-expanded attributes.\n# Given a keyboard-only user is on mobile with the drawer closed, when they Tab to the hamburger button, then it is announced as Open navigation menu with aria-expanded false.\n# Given a keyboard-only user opens the mobile drawer, when the drawer is active, then the close icon is announced as Close navigation menu with aria-expanded true.\n# Given a keyboard-only user is interacting with the nav on any device, when they interact with any element, then all interactions are achievable without a mouse."
  }
}"}

