content.content:
{"result":"{
  "id": "19207",
  "key": "SFCC-747",
  "summary": "Logo Masthead – PWA Kit Component",
  "customfield_10231": {
    "value": "*Structure — Desktop*\n1. Given a shopper visits any page on desktop, when the page loads, then the Logo Masthead component renders the Zimmermann wordmark left-aligned in the header at the desktop size, consistent with the Figma design.\n2. Given a shopper visits any page on desktop, when the logo renders, then it sits in its own row below the top-level navigation bar, not inline with the nav items.\n\n*Structure — Tablet*\n3. Given a shopper visits any page on tablet, when the page loads, then the Logo Masthead component renders the Zimmermann wordmark left-aligned in the header at the tablet size, consistent with the Figma design.\n4. Given a shopper visits any page on tablet, when the logo renders, then it sits in its own row below the top-level navigation bar, consistent with desktop behaviour.\n\n*Structure — Mobile*\n5. Given a shopper visits any page on mobile, when the page loads, then the Logo Masthead component renders the Zimmermann wordmark left-aligned in the header at the mobile size, consistent with the Figma design.\n\n*Link Behaviour*\n6. Given a shopper clicks the logo on any page, when the click is registered, then they are navigated to the homepage of the active storefront.\n7. Given a shopper is already on the homepage, when they click the logo, then the page reloads or no navigation occurs — the logo does not navigate away from the homepage unexpectedly.\n\n*Accessibility*\n8. Given a screen reader user navigates to the logo, when the logo receives focus, then the logo image is announced as Zimmermann via alt text on the img element, or aria-label on an inline SVG with role=img.\n9. Given a screen reader user navigates to the logo link, when the link receives focus, then the link is announced as Zimmermann, go to homepage so the destination is clear.\n10. Given a keyboard-only user is navigating the header, when they Tab to the logo, then the logo link is the first focusable element in the header, before any navigation items or icons.\n11. Given a keyboard-only user tabs to the logo link, when it receives focus, then a visible focus ring is displayed that meets WCAG 2.1 AA contrast requirements (minimum 3:1 ratio against adjacent colours)."
  }
}"}

